\details{
\subsection{The Estimand}{

The ATE, ATT and ATC are averages of the conditional effect over the
covariates of the units averaged over, computed within each posterior draw.
This is sometimes called the mixed average treatment effect. The covariates
are treated as fixed, so the interval reflects uncertainty about the outcome
model but not the further variation a population average would carry, and
the units' observed outcomes are not conditioned on, as they would be for a
sample average of individual effects. Likewise, \code{estimand = "CATE"} reports
the expected effect at each unit's covariates rather than the unit's own
effect, which depends on how its two potential outcomes are associated and is
not identified. See \code{vignette("causal")} for the distinction between these
estimands.
}

\subsection{Setting \code{type}}{

A varying coefficient is a contrast on the link scale: on a
\code{binomial("logit")} fit, \code{coef(object)} is a per-unit difference in log odds,
and the average of those is the average conditional log odds ratio rather than
the marginal one a treatment question usually asks for. The default is
therefore \code{type = "response"}, where every unit's contrast is on the scale the
response is measured on and averaging them gives the marginal effect.

Setting \code{type = "link"} is the right choice for looking at how the effect varies,
since that is the scale the forest models it on, and the wrong one for
reporting an average.
}

\subsection{Setting \code{comparison}}{

For \code{"ratio"}, \code{"lnratio"}, \code{"or"} and \code{"lnor"} the potential outcomes are
averaged over units first and contrasted afterward, which gives the marginal
ratio. With \code{estimand = "CATE"} there is no averaging to do, so a ratio reported there is
a conditional ratio and the \code{print()} method says so.

For the same reason \code{"or"} and \code{"lnor"} are not each other's \code{exp()} and
\code{log()}. Each summarizes the posterior of the quantity it names, and a
posterior mean does not survive a nonlinear transformation: \code{"or"} reports the
mean of the odds ratio and \code{"lnor"} the mean of its logarithm, which
exponentiates to something smaller. Report whichever scale the interval
should be symmetric on, which for a ratio is usually the log. The same holds
of \code{"ratio"} against \code{"lnratio"}.
}

\subsection{Setting \code{focal}}{

With a binary treatment and no \code{focal}, which level is the treated one is
worked out from the treatment's values by the rules
\pkgfun{WeightIt}{weightit} uses, so that the same variable is read the same
way by both packages. In order: a \code{logical} treatment is treated at \code{TRUE}; a
\code{numeric} one is untreated at 0; a \code{character} or \code{factor} one whose levels
parse as numbers is untreated at 0, or else treated at the largest; and
otherwise the conventional names are matched, \code{"t"}, \code{"tr"}, \code{"treat"},
\code{"treated"} and \code{"exposed"} against \code{"c"}, \code{"co"}, \code{"ctrl"}, \code{"control"} and
\code{"unexposed"}. When none of those applies the second level is taken as the
treated one and a message says so, that being the case where the guess can be
wrong. \code{"ATC"} takes the other level as its focal group, so both estimands
average over the group \code{focal} names and only the default differs.
}

\subsection{Multi-category Treatments}{

There is nothing to work out from the values, so the \code{focal} argument is required for
\code{"ATT"} and \code{"ATC"}, and those two then name the same estimand: the effect
among the units in the level named.

Every pairwise contrast is computed. Which ones are shown is a display
choice, made by \code{contrasts} in the \code{print()} method: with a focal group the
default is to show only the contrasts involving it, matching how
\pkgfun{WeightIt}{weightit} and \pkgfun{cobalt}{bal.tab} use \code{focal}.

The effect of a continuous treatment is a slope or a dose-response curve
rather than a contrast of levels, and the \CRANpkg{adrftools} package has
tools for summarizing and visualizing one.
}
}
